<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Opobo</h1><p class="st-meta">1870 CE – 1887 CE · capital Opobo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The trading state founded by Jaja, a former slave who broke away from Bonny and dominated the palm oil trade of the eastern Niger Delta until his deportation in 1887.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaja-of-opobo">Jaja</a>, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igboland">Igboland</a> and sold as a boy to Bonny, rose to head the Anna Pepple trading house of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bonny-kingdom">Bonny Kingdom</a>. After a civil war between rival houses, he left Bonny in 1869 with his followers and in 1870 founded Opobo on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imo-river">Imo</a> river, placing himself between Bonny and the interior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> markets.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Opobo was a city-state built around trading houses, each with its own canoe fleet and dependants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaja-of-opobo">Jaja</a> controlled access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> producers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a>, dealt directly with British firms and even shipped oil to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> on his own account. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> recognised him as king in 1873. He kept missionaries out and maintained traditional religion while sending his children to be educated abroad.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaja-of-opobo">Jaja</a> refused to let British traders bypass him. In 1887 the British consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-johnston">Harry Johnston</a> lured him aboard a ship and deported him; he died in 1891. Opobo was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/oil-rivers-protectorate">Oil Rivers Protectorate</a>, and its king remains a traditional ruler.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-opobo.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Opobo" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: MediaMOF · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_statue_of_Jaja_of_Opobo_at_Jaja_of_Opobo_Palace,_Opobo,_Rivers_state3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1870 CE – 1887 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Opobo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jaja</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Igbo and Ijaw traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ibani (Ijaw), Igbo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Nigeria</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Opobo</dd></dl></aside></div>
